  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After Senya hints that he must report the injury to the police unless he is paid for his silence, Mikhail promises to settle up with him. In the restaurant, a polite woman orders a Sex on the Beach cocktail from Nastya while Max frets over his mistake.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надо в полицию сообщать. Понял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Не понял. За молчание, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ничего, вы не беспокойтесь. Мы решим это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Добрый день. Готовы делать заказ?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Да, пожалуйста, коктейль.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Секс на пляже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика сильно расстроилась.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Как думаешь, я проверку сорвал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Конечно сорвал, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Костя, один коктейль Секс на пляже!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, что она заказала?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Коктейль Секс на пляже.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надо в полицию сообщать. Понял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have to report it to the police. Understood?' The grammar packs 'need' and 'understood' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Не понял. За молчание, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I did not understand. For silence, yes?' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'understood' to negate the statement.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ничего, вы не беспокойтесь. Мы решим это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is nothing, do not worry. We will solve this.' The meaning hinges on 'nothing' working together with 'this' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Добрый день. Готовы делать заказ?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good afternoon. Are you ready to place an order?' In Russian, the idea of 'good' flows naturally into 'order' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Да, пожалуйста, коктейль.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, please, a cocktail.' Notice how Russian expresses 'yes' and 'cocktail' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Секс на пляже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Sex on the Beach.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'sex' before landing on 'beach'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика сильно расстроилась.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vika got very upset.' The grammar packs 'Vika' and 'got upset' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Как думаешь, я проверку сорвал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you think, did I ruin the inspection?' Max anchors the line on 'how', while 'ruined' completes the thought. Nastya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Конечно сорвал, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course you ruined it, Max.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'of course' before landing on 'Max'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Костя, один коктейль Секс на пляже!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kostya, one Sex on the Beach cocktail!' The meaning hinges on 'Kostya' working together with 'beach' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, что она заказала?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, what did she order?' The grammar packs 'wait' and 'to order' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Nastya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Коктейль Секс на пляже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A Sex on the Beach cocktail.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'cocktail' and finishing on 'beach'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
